introduction:
End-of-life lithium-ion batteries from electric vehicles should be disassembled so that their materials can be recovered [1]. The work is hazardous. Packs weigh about 450 kg, carry up to 800 V, arrive in an unknown state of charge, and can enter thermal runaway or electrocute workers [2]. Gantry automation handles part of the task [2, 3], but recycling facilities are built around human workers, so a full-size humanoid that fits into the existing cell without ramps or fixtures is attractive (Fig. 1). Such a humanoid is also a 70 kg machine that must actively balance, and when it fails, it usually falls. Under geometric scaling, mass grows with the cube of height, and both the joint torque needed to support it and the fall energy grow with the fourth power. The 1.78 m H1-2 has three times the knee torque of the 1.32 m, 35 kg Unitree G1 (360 vs. 120 Nm) [4, 5], close to the (1.78/1.32)4≈3.3(1.78/1.32)^{4}\approx 3.3 that this scaling predicts, so the same control error produces roughly three times the joint torque and fall energy.

This extended abstract describes the safety-relevant parts of Golem (Generalized Open Library of Embodied Modules) [6], an open-source ROS 2 stack for the H1-2 in which lower-body control, upper-body control, navigation, perception, and grasping are interchangeable modules behind fixed interfaces. Our contributions are:

the runtime safety layer between the control modules and the motors, and deployment observations showing that its own limits can trigger a hazardous stop (Sec. III);

the emergency stop, push trials in which the robot lost balance and needed it, and an analysis of why stopping a humanoid is itself a hazard that industrial stop categories do not address (Sec. IV);

a case for graduated force authority: force limits do not settle whether a force should be applied, so, drawing on developmental psychology and research on responsibility, we argue that force authority be widened in stages and tied to a responsible party (Sec. V).

Learning from demonstrations reduces the effort required to program robots for complex or variable tasks [1, 2]. This is increasingly relevant for large visuomotor policies, whose performance depends on the scale and quality of their demonstration data [3].
Collaborative efforts such as Open X-Embodiment [4] aggregate standardized manipulation data across robot platforms, tasks, and institutions using traditional teleoperation approaches, which are typically time-consuming. This limitation has motivated the creation of handheld data collection devices that enable rapid data collection. Universal Manipulation Interfaces (UMIs) support portable, robot-free data collection and thereby decouple data acquisition from continuous access to the target robot [5, 6, 7]. These devices have also gained popularity in the tactile manipulation community, where collecting large-scale datasets remains challenging due to the inherent sparsity of tactile sensor signals [8, 9, 10].

Despite the scalability and speed-up they offer, the inherent robot-free nature of these approaches means that it becomes difficult to observe the validity of the trajectories which they generate. While an intuition for robot-compatible motion can be learned through practice, the lack of feedback necessitates careful post-processing to filter out any unusable data. Furthermore, most existing methods are designed around custom-built robotic grippers, which makes them unsuitable for applications that require more robust grippers.

Another underexplored potential of UMI devices is that they can serve as a more intuitive interface for teleoperation, given their similar appearances and kinematics to target grippers. However, most existing UMI designs lack a way to transmit contact information at time of grasp, which makes it difficult to regulate grasp forces in such scenarios.

To address these challenges, we present the Robotiq Universal Manipulation Interface (RUMI), a handheld platform matched to the nonlinear kinematics, finger attachments, and wrist-camera viewpoint of the Robotiq 2F-85. A scissor-like input drives the coupled fingers, while a rotary encoder measures the resulting grasp coordinate. Interchangeable fingers support force/torque and vision-based tactile sensing for contact-rich demonstrations. During simulated and real-robot teleoperation, a contact-triggered blocker provides low-resistance aperture control before contact and converts the subsequent squeeze into a measured force command. By integrating the handheld pose tracking into a virtual reality (VR) headset, we can project a digital shadow of the target robot platform in augmented reality (AR) fashion during data collection, allowing the operator to recognize infeasible poses while recording rather than filtering them only after collection.

In summary, we make the following contributions:

RUMI, a Robotiq 2F-85-matched handheld interface with nonlinear actuation and interchangeable fingertips;

An AR pipeline for visualizing and validating robot configurations during robot-free data collection;

A haptic feedback mechanism which allows for gripper force control during simulated and real teleoperation;

Releasing the device design and software framework as well as a dataset of demonstrations featuring force/torque and vision-based tactile sensor data.

1. RUMI, a Robotiq 2F-85-matched handheld interface with nonlinear actuation and interchangeable fingertips;

2. An AR pipeline for visualizing and validating robot configurations during robot-free data collection;

3. A haptic feedback mechanism which allows for gripper force control during simulated and real teleoperation;

4. Releasing the device design and software framework as well as a dataset of demonstrations featuring force/torque and vision-based tactile sensor data.
